content.content:
9:- `anon`: a request made with the public API key and no login.
10:- `authenticated`: a logged-in request. The plugins log in as their space's "anonymous" account (`agent_type = 'anonymous'`), so they use `authenticated`, not `anon`.
13:`anon` reads almost nothing today.
33:- Its grants: `SELECT` only. `anon` holds it for public access (see [Grants made for public access](#grants-made-for-public-access)). It returns no rows to `anon` today.
47:- `my_pseudo_accounts` returns `dg_account`, so it has to stay a definer view. Any `anon` grant on `PlatformAccount` has to use the same column list.
70:| `anon`          | nothing                                | nothing           | nothing   |
74:We set these ourselves because the Supabase image's defaults change between versions. Image 17.11.0.018 gives client roles only `TRUNCATE`, `REFERENCES`, `TRIGGER` and `MAINTAIN` on new tables, and nothing on new sequences or functions, so a database built on it from migrations that relied on the image was unusable (ENG-2466).
81:`base.sql` also revokes `PUBLIC` from the global function defaults of the `postgres` role. Postgres grants EXECUTE on every new function to `PUBLIC`, and `anon` inherits it. A schema-level default cannot remove a global one, so this also applies to functions that `postgres` creates in other schemas.
83:The schema files declare only the exceptions to these defaults, next to the object, with a `--` comment saying which client or policy needs them: every `anon` grant, and every privilege removed from `authenticated` or `service_role`. Functions that are not `SECURITY DEFINER` run as the caller under RLS, so they are granted to `anon` as a group, at the end of their schema file. Don't write `GRANT ALL ... TO authenticated`: on a table, it includes `TRUNCATE`, which RLS doesn't limit.
85:`CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION` keeps a function's grants, but `DROP FUNCTION` followed by `CREATE FUNCTION` loses them. A migration that recreates a function must repeat its exceptions, such as an `anon` grant. Dropping a view also drops the functions that return its row type, such as `author_of_concept` and `author_of_content` for `my_accounts`, so they need their grants repeated too. `REVOKE ALL ON TABLE` also removes the table's column grants, such as those on `PlatformAccount`.
97:### Functions `anon` can execute
103:| `my_space_ids`     | `Space` select policy; `anon` can read `Space`. Public access: the `my_*` views and the other select policies                                                                            |
108:`in_space` is not executable by `anon`. Only write paths reach it as the caller, and `anon`'s reads use `my_space_ids`.
110:`anon` holds only `SELECT` on the contributor tables, and its reads fail with a permission error on `ResourceAccess` until public access adds the grants below.
114:`anon` will read public nodes, their files and their authors (ENG-1837). The "Public access" entries above, and `anon`'s `SELECT` on `Space`, the contributor tables and the views, are grants made ahead of that work. `anon` can't execute the functions that write, except `get_secret_token`. Keep them: rule 1 means that removing one fails those queries with a permission error. ENG-1837 also has to grant `anon` `SELECT` on `Concept`, `Content`, `Document`, `FileReference` and `ResourceAccess`, and open `storage_select_assets_access` to `anon`, which today applies only to `authenticated`.
124:- `my_groups` reads `auth.users` (`auth_users_exposed` and `security_definer_view`). Groups are rows in `auth.users`, with the synthetic email `{group name}@groups.discoursegraphs.com` set by the `create-group` edge function. The view returns only the id and name of the groups the current user belongs to; `anon` gets no rows. It must run as its owner, because clients can't read `auth.users`. This stays.
127:- `anon_security_definer_function_executable` for the four functions in the table above.
143:- New table: enable RLS, because `authenticated` can read and write it by default. Write policies for each operation, and grant `anon` only what anonymous readers need. Add visibility scenarios to `features/`, like `features/conceptAccess.feature`.
147:- New function that `anon` reaches, directly, through a policy or through a view: grant it to `anon` in its schema file and in the migration.
149:- New or changed view: keep existing names and columns, and mirror the table's access rules. Revoke `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` from `authenticated`, and grant `SELECT` to `anon` if anonymous readers need it.
162:- ENG-2442: function grants made explicit, and new functions no longer executable by `anon`.
166:- ENG-2466: default privileges set in `base.sql` instead of taken from the Supabase image, and every existing object's grants made explicit. Client roles lost `TRUNCATE`, `REFERENCES`, `TRIGGER` and `MAINTAIN`, write privileges on the views, and `UPDATE` on sequences. `anon` lost its writes on `Space` and `EXECUTE` on the functions that write. `authenticated` lost `INSERT` on `Space`, since only create-space creates spaces, and `space_insert_policy` was removed.
18:CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.get_secret_token(token VARCHAR) RETURNS JSONB
19-SECURITY DEFINER
20-SET search_path = ''
21-LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
22-DECLARE
23-v_payload JSONB;
24-v_one_time_use BOOLEAN;
25-BEGIN
26-    DELETE FROM public.secret_token WHERE expiry_date < now();
27-    DELETE FROM public.secret_token WHERE id=token AND one_time_use = true RETURNING payload INTO v_payload;
28-    IF v_payload IS NULL THEN
29-        SELECT payload INTO v_payload FROM public.secret_token WHERE id=token;
30-    END IF;
--
36:GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.get_secret_token TO anon;
37-
38-ALTER TABLE public.secret_token OWNER TO "postgres";
39-
40-
41-ALTER TABLE public.secret_token ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
42-DROP POLICY IF EXISTS concept_policy ON public.secret_token;
43-DROP POLICY IF EXISTS concept_select_policy ON public.secret_token;
44-CREATE POLICY concept_select_policy ON public.secret_token FOR SELECT USING (creator = (SELECT auth.uid()));
45-DROP POLICY IF EXISTS concept_delete_policy ON public.secret_token;
