74:We set these ourselves because the Supabase image's defaults change between versions. Image 17.11.0.018 gives client roles only `TRUNCATE`, `REFERENCES`, `TRIGGER` and `MAINTAIN` on new tables, and nothing on new sequences or functions, so a database built on it from migrations that relied on the image was unusable (ENG-2466).
81:`base.sql` also revokes `PUBLIC` from the global function defaults of the `postgres` role. Postgres grants EXECUTE on every new function to `PUBLIC`, and `anon` inherits it. A schema-level default cannot remove a global one, so this also applies to functions that `postgres` creates in other schemas.
83:The schema files declare only the exceptions to these defaults, next to the object, with a `--` comment saying which client or policy needs them: every `anon` grant, and every privilege removed from `authenticated` or `service_role`. Functions that are not `SECURITY DEFINER` run as the caller under RLS, so they are granted to `anon` as a group, at the end of their schema file. Don't write `GRANT ALL ... TO authenticated`: on a table, it includes `TRUNCATE`, which RLS doesn't limit.
85:`CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION` keeps a function's grants, but `DROP FUNCTION` followed by `CREATE FUNCTION` loses them. A migration that recreates a function must repeat its exceptions, such as an `anon` grant. Dropping a view also drops the functions that return its row type, such as `author_of_concept` and `author_of_content` for `my_accounts`, so they need their grants repeated too. `REVOKE ALL ON TABLE` also removes the table's column grants, such as those on `PlatformAccount`.
